/**
 * Wrangler configuration template for the WATCHDOG Worker.
 *
 * Copy to wrangler.watchdog.jsonc and fill in the real alert address. The real
 * file is gitignored; secrets are set with `wrangler secret put`, never here.
 * `scripts/bootstrap-config.mjs` copies this into place on install, and
 * `npm run check:config` compares the two in both directions.
 *
 *   npx wrangler deploy -c wrangler.watchdog.jsonc
 *   npx wrangler secret put OPERATOR_TOKEN -c wrangler.watchdog.jsonc
 *
 * A SECOND WORKER RATHER THAN A SECOND CRON ON THE SITE. A watcher that runs
 * inside the thing it watches dies with it, which is the argument
 * .github/workflows/health.yml used to reject every in-Worker design. A
 * separate Worker keeps that property against everything except a
 * Cloudflare-wide outage, in which case the site is down anyway. The whole
 * ruling, and the measurement that forced the transport, are in
 * workers/watchdog.ts.
 */
{
	"$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
	"name": "dustinedwards-watchdog",
	"compatibility_date": "2026-09-01",
	"main": "./workers/watchdog.ts",
	// NO PUBLIC SURFACE. This Worker exports `scheduled` and nothing else, so a
	// workers.dev route would answer errors to anyone who found it and would be
	// a second way in to a Worker holding the operator token. A watchdog has no
	// business being reachable.
	"workers_dev": false,
	// Same posture as the site config, and for the same privacy reason: an
	// invocation log is enriched with the request context. This Worker has no
	// requests, so the setting costs nothing here and states the account-wide
	// stance rather than leaving one Worker as the exception nobody notices.
	"observability": {
		"enabled": true,
		"logs": {
			"invocation_logs": false
		}
	},
	// EVERY FIFTEEN MINUTES, and this cron is now the ONE owner of the poll
	// interval. `HEALTH_POLL_INTERVAL_SECONDS` in app/lib/health/snapshot.mjs
	// decides when the home page calls its health verdict stale, and
	// `check:invariants` section 25 parses this line and fails if the two stop
	// meaning the same number of seconds. It used to parse health.yml's cron;
	// that workflow is now the hourly second opinion and no longer sets the pace.
	"triggers": {
		"crons": ["*/15 * * * *"]
	},
	// THE ONLY WAY THIS WORKER CAN REACH THE SITE. Measured 2026-08-29: a global
	// fetch() to the site's own URL answers 404 with Cloudflare error 1042, the
	// Worker-to-Worker-on-one-zone restriction, while a control fetch to
	// example.com returns 200. Custom Domains are the documented alternative and
	// this site has none until the DNS cutover. Carries no account-scoped id, so
	// this line is complete as written.
	"services": [
		{
			"binding": "SITE",
			"service": "dustinedwards"
		}
	],
	// WHAT THE LAST FIRING SAW, so this one can tell a CHANGE from a repeat.
	//
	// The cron is stateless by nature: it fires, reads, and forgets. That is how
	// one condition red for an afternoon produced 27 identical emails. The
	// dedupe needs exactly one fact carried between firings, and KV is the right
	// shape for it: a single small value, written at most once per firing, read
	// once, with no ordering or transaction requirement.
	//
	// EVENTUAL CONSISTENCY IS FINE HERE and the numbers are why: KV settles well
	// inside a minute and this cron fires every fifteen, so a write is long
	// settled before the next read. A Durable Object would buy strict ordering
	// this does not need and add a class of failure a monitor should not have.
	//
	// THE SITE'S NAMESPACE, not a new one. The state is one key under a
	// `watchdog:` prefix; a second namespace would be a second thing to create
	// in RECOVERY.md for one value.
	"kv_namespaces": [
		{
			"binding": "APP_KV",
			"id": "00000000000000000000000000000000"
		}
	],
	// Cloudflare Email Sending. `dustinedwards.info` is onboarded (measured
	// 2026-08-29 with `wrangler email sending list`) and the alert address is an
	// already-verified Email Routing destination, which the docs say is free on
	// any plan and counts against no quota. Unrestricted rather than pinned with
	// `destination_address`, because the generated `SendEmail` type requires
	// `to`, so a pinned binding would mean the address written twice. The var
	// below is the one owner. Carries no account-scoped id.
	"send_email": [
		{
			"name": "EMAIL"
		}
	],
	// WHERE THE ALERT LANDS, and the one statement of it.
	//
	// A VAR RATHER THAN A SECRET, on the CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID precedent in the
	// site config: an inbox address is not a credential, and guarding it with
	// check:secrets would spend that gate's signal on a value that grants
	// nothing. It is still kept OUT OF GIT, because this repo is meant to be
	// copied as a template for further sites and a personal address in a tracked
	// config is exactly what leaks on the day it is copied.
	//
	// The placeholder is on example.com, which RFC 2606 reserves and which can
	// therefore never be a real inbox. `check:config` asserts the example
	// carries that reserved domain and does NOT carry the real value.
	"vars": {
		"ALERT_EMAIL": "alerts@example.com"
	}
}
